    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_298",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nTakahashi has a playlist with N songs.\nSong i (1 \\leq i \\leq N) lasts T_i seconds.\nTakahashi has started random play of the playlist at time 0.\nRandom play repeats the following: choose one song from the N songs with equal probability and play that song to the end.\nHere, songs are played continuously: once a song ends, the next chosen song starts immediately.\nThe same song can be chosen consecutively.\nFind the probability that song 1 is being played (X + 0.5) seconds after time 0, modulo 998244353.\n\nHow to print a probability modulo 998244353\nIt can be proved that the probability to be found in this problem is always a rational number.\nAlso, the constraints of this problem guarantee that when the probability to be found is expressed as an irreducible fraction \\frac{{y}}{{x}}, x is not divisible by 998244353.\nThen, there is a unique integer z between 0 and 998244352, inclusive, such that xz \\equiv y \\pmod{{998244353}}. Report this z.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN X\nT_1 T_2 \\ldots T_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the probability, modulo 998244353, that the first song in the playlist is being played (X+0.5) seconds after time 0.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2 \\leq N\\leq 10^3\n- 0 \\leq X\\leq 10^4\n- 1 \\leq T_i\\leq 10^4\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3 6\n3 5 6\n\nSample Output 1\n\n369720131\n\nSong 1 will be playing 6.5 seconds after time 0 if songs are played in one of the following orders.\n\n- Song 1 \\to Song 1 \\to Song 1\n- Song 2 \\to Song 1 \n- Song 3 \\to Song 1 \n\nThe probability that one of these occurs is \\frac{{7}}{{27}}.\nWe have 369720131\\times 27\\equiv 7 \\pmod{{998244353}}, so you should print 369720131.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n5 0\n1 2 1 2 1\n\nSample Output 2\n\n598946612\n\n0.5 seconds after time 0, the first song to be played is still playing, so the sought probability is \\frac{{1}}{{5}}.\nNote that different songs may have the same length.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n5 10000\n1 2 3 4 5\n\nSample Output 3\n\n586965467",
    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve the problem, we need to calculate the probability that song 1 is being played at (X + 0.5) seconds after time 0. This involves understanding the distribution of song play times and how they align with the given time X.\n\nFirst, we'll define the function `solve` which will:\n1. Parse the input to get the number of songs (N), the specific time (X), and the list of song durations (T).\n2. Calculate the cumulative sum of song durations to determine how song plays align with the time X.\n3. Adjust the probability calculation considering the modular arithmetic constraints given by the problem.\n\nHere's the step-by-step breakdown of the function:\n\n1. **Input Parsing**: Read the integers N and X, followed by the list of song durations T.\n2. **Cumulative Sum Calculation**: Compute the cumulative sum of song durations. This helps in determining how many times the cumulative duration surpasses X.\n3. **Probability Calculation**:\n   - Adjust X to account for the half-second offset by adding 0.5.\n   - For each song duration, calculate how many times the song could have been playing around time X.\n   - Compute the probability using modular arithmetic to ensure the result fits within the constraints of modulo 998244353.\n\nThe function will then print the result after calculating the probability.\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    N = int(data[0])\n    X = int(data[1])\n    T = list(map(int, data[2:2+N]))\n    \n    MOD = 998244353\n    \n    # Calculate cumulative sums of song durations\n    cumulative_sums = [0] * (N + 1)\n    for i in range(1, N + 1):\n        cumulative_sums[i] = cumulative_sums[i - 1] + T[i - 1]\n    \n    # Initialize probability\n    probability = 1\n    \n    # Calculate the probability that song 1 is being played at (X + 0.5) seconds\n    for i in range(1, N + 1):\n        # Count how many times song i could have ended before or at time X\n        # and started again so that it's playing at time X + 0.5\n        if X < cumulative_sums[i - 1]:\n            # If X is less than the cumulative sum of times before song i,\n            # it means song i has not yet started at time X\n            continue\n        \n        # Number of times song i could be playing at time X + 0.5\n        count = (X - cumulative_sums[i - 1] + 1) // T[i - 1]\n        \n        # Update the probability\n        probability = probability * (count + 1) % MOD\n    \n    # Since we need to find the modular inverse of the total number of songs\n    # to get the actual probability modulo 998244353\n    total_songs_inverse = pow(N, MOD - 2, MOD)\n    probability = probability * total_songs_inverse % MOD\n    \n    # Print the result\n    print(probability)\n\n```",
