      "generated_answer": "To create a function that returns the length of the longest valid substring of a given string `word`, excluding any substrings found in the array `forbidden`, we can use a sliding window approach. Here's the function body without invoking it:\n\n```python\ndef longest_valid_substring(word, forbidden):\n    max_length = 0\n    forbidden_set = set(forbidden)  # To allow O(1) lookups\n    forbidden_lengths = {f: len(f) for f in forbidden}  # Store lengths for quick lookup\n\n    # This will keep track of the starting index of the current valid substring\n    start_index = 0\n\n    # Iterate through the string\n    for end_index in range(len(word)):\n        # Check each forbidden word if it starts at the current end_index\n        for forbidden_word, forbidden_len in forbidden_lengths.items():\n            # If a forbidden word starts at the current index, move the start index\n            if end_index >= forbidden_len and word[end_index - forbidden_len + 1:end_index + 1] == forbidden_word:\n                start_index = end_index - forbidden_len + 1\n\n        # Update the maximum length found\n        max_length = max(max_length, end_index - start_index + 1)\n\n    return max_length\n```\n\nThis function uses a sliding window approach to keep track of the longest valid substring. It iterates through the `word` string, and for each character, it checks if a forbidden substring begins at that character. If a forbidden substring is detected, the starting index of the current valid substring is moved right past the forbidden substring. Throughout this process, the function keeps track of the maximum length of any valid substring found.",
