    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Below is a Python function that calculates the total number of ways to distribute `n` candies among 3 children such that no child gets more than `limit` candies:\n\n```python\ndef count_ways(n, limit):\n    # Helper function to count the ways to distribute candies\n    def count(candies, child1, child2, child3):\n        if candies < 0:\n            return 0\n        if child1 == child2 == child3 == 0:\n            return 1\n        ways = 0\n        # Distribute candies to the first child without exceeding the limit\n        for x in range(min(limit, candies) + 1):\n            ways += count(candies - x, child1 - x, child2, child3)\n        # Distribute candies to the second child without exceeding the limit\n        for x in range(min(limit, candies) + 1):\n            ways += count(candies - x, child1, child2 - x, child3)\n        # Distribute candies to the third child without exceeding the limit\n        for x in range(min(limit, candies) + 1):\n            ways += count(candies - x, child1, child2, child3 - x)\n        return ways\n\n    return count(n, limit, limit, limit)\n```\n\nThis function uses recursion to explore all possible distributions of candies, ensuring that no child receives more than `limit` candies. It defines an inner helper function `count` that recursively calculates the number of valid distributions for a given number of candies left and current distribution among the children. The recursion stops when the number of candies left is negative (invalid case) or when all children have no candies left (valid case). The function then sums up all valid distributions.\n\nNote that this is a brute-force approach, and there are more efficient ways to solve this problem using dynamic programming or combinatorial mathematics. However, the code provided meets the requirements specified in the prompt.",
