    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_493",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a string word of size n, and an integer k such that k divides n.\nIn one operation, you can pick any two indices i and j, that are divisible by k, then replace the substring of length k starting at i with the substring of length k starting at j. That is, replace the substring word[i..i + k - 1] with the substring word[j..j + k - 1].\nReturn the minimum number of operations required to make word k-periodic.\nWe say that word is k-periodic if there is some string s of length k such that word can be obtained by concatenating s an arbitrary number of times. For example, if word == “ababab”, then word is 2-periodic for s = \"ab\".\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: word = \"leetcodeleet\", k = 4\nOutput: 1\nExplanation:\nWe can obtain a 4-periodic string by picking i = 4 and j = 0. After this operation, word becomes equal to \"leetleetleet\".\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: word = \"leetcoleet\", k = 2\nOutput: 3\nExplanation:\nWe can obtain a 2-periodic string by applying the operations in the table below.\n\n\n\ni\nj\nword\n\n\n0\n2\netetcoleet\n\n\n4\n0\netetetleet\n\n\n6\n0\netetetetet\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= n == word.length <= 10^5\n1 <= k <= word.length\nk divides word.length.\nword consists only of lowercase English letters.",
    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Here is a Python function that calculates the minimum number of operations required to make a given string `word` k-periodic, as per the provided prompt:\n\n```python\ndef min_operations_to_k_periodic(word, k):\n    n = len(word)\n    # Initialize the minimum operations to a large number\n    min_operations = n // k\n    \n    # Iterate over possible starting points for the repeating unit\n    for start in range(k):\n        # Initialize a dictionary to store the frequency of each substring\n        substr_freq = {}\n        # Initialize a variable to track the number of operations for the current start\n        operations = 0\n        \n        # Iterate over the word in steps of k to consider each k-length substring\n        for i in range(start, n, k):\n            substr = word[i:i+k]\n            if substr in substr_freq:\n                # If the substring has been seen before, increment its frequency\n                substr_freq[substr] += 1\n            else:\n                # Otherwise, add it to the dictionary with a frequency of 1\n                substr_freq[substr] = 1\n                \n        # Calculate the number of operations needed to make all blocks the same as the most frequent block\n        most_frequent_count = max(substr_freq.values())\n        operations = (n // k) - most_frequent_count\n        \n        # Update the minimum operations if the current one is lower\n        min_operations = min(min_operations, operations)\n\n    return min_operations\n```\n\nThis function loops over each possible starting index of the k-length substrings, counts the frequency of each substring, and then calculates how many operations are required to make all k-length substrings the same as the most frequent one. The minimum number of operations across all starting indices is returned.",
