      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we can define a function `sum_of_imbalance_numbers` that calculates the sum of imbalance numbers for all subarrays of the given array `nums`. The function would iterate through all possible subarrays, sort each one, and then count the number of indices that satisfy the imbalance condition. Below is the function body without invocation:\n\n```python\ndef sum_of_imbalance_numbers(nums):\n    def get_imbalance_number(subarray):\n        sorted_subarray = sorted(subarray)\n        imbalance_number = 0\n        for i in range(len(sorted_subarray) - 1):\n            if sorted_subarray[i + 1] - sorted_subarray[i] > 1:\n                imbalance_number += 1\n        return imbalance_number\n    \n    total_imbalance = 0\n    n = len(nums)\n    for start in range(n):\n        for end in range(start + 1, n + 1):\n            subarray = nums[start:end]\n            total_imbalance += get_imbalance_number(subarray)\n    return total_imbalance\n```\n\nThis function defines a nested helper function `get_imbalance_number` to calculate the imbalance number for a given subarray. Then it iterates through all subarrays of `nums` using two nested loops, calculates the imbalance number for each subarray, and adds it to the total imbalance. The outer loop selects the starting index of the subarray, while the inner loop selects the ending index (exclusive).",
