    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Here is a Python function that calculates the total number of continuous subarrays according to the given prompt:\n\n```python\ndef count_continuous_subarrays(nums):\n    # Initialize the count of continuous subarrays\n    count = 0\n    # Iterate through the array\n    for i in range(len(nums)):\n        # Initialize the length of the current valid subarray\n        length = 1\n        # Check for continuous subarrays starting from index i\n        for j in range(i+1, len(nums)):\n            # If the difference between elements is more than 2, break the loop\n            if abs(nums[j] - nums[i]) > 2:\n                break\n            # Otherwise, increase the length of the current valid subarray\n            length += 1\n        # Add the number of subarrays ending at index i\n        count += length * (length + 1) // 2\n    return count\n``` \n\nThis function uses a brute-force approach to count the number of continuous subarrays. For each starting index `i`, it checks all possible ending indices `j` to see if they form a valid subarray according to the given condition. If the subarray is valid, it increments the `length` variable. The number of subarrays ending at each index is the sum of the first `length` natural numbers, which is calculated using the formula `length * (length + 1) // 2`. After checking all possible subarrays for a starting index, it moves to the next index. Finally, the function returns the total count.",
