      "generated_answer": "Below is the Python function body that accomplishes the task described in the prompt without invoking the function at the end:\n\n```python\ndef min_cost_string(s):\n    # Initialize a dictionary to keep track of the frequency of each character\n    char_count = {chr(i): 0 for i in range(ord('a'), ord('z') + 1)}\n    # List to store the characters that are not '?' in the original string\n    fixed_chars = [c for c in s if c != '?']\n    # List to store the positions of '?' in the original string\n    question_marks = [i for i, c in enumerate(s) if c == '?']\n\n    # Iterate over the fixed characters to update their frequencies\n    for char in fixed_chars:\n        char_count[char] += 1\n\n    # Replace '?' with the lexicographically smallest character that minimizes cost\n    for i in question_marks:\n        # Find the smallest character with a frequency of 0 or 1\n        for char in sorted(char_count):\n            if char_count[char] == 0 or char_count[char] == 1 and all(c != char for c in fixed_chars[:i]):\n                s = s[:i] + char + s[i+1:]\n                char_count[char] += 1\n                break\n\n    return s\n```\n\nThis function goes through the input string `s`, and for each '?' encountered, it replaces it with the lexicographically smallest character that will not increase the cost. It does this by maintaining a count of each letter and by making sure that it doesn't use a letter that would cause an unnecessary increase in the cost for any position in the string.",
