    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Here's the Python function that meets the criteria specified in the prompt:\n\n```python\ndef is_prime(num):\n    if num <= 1:\n        return False\n    if num <= 3:\n        return True\n    if num % 2 == 0 or num % 3 == 0:\n        return False\n    i = 5\n    while i * i <= num:\n        if num % i == 0 or num % (i + 2) == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 6\n    return True\n\ndef prime_number_pairs(n):\n    prime_pairs = []\n    for x in range(2, n // 2 + 1):\n        y = n - x\n        if is_prime(x) and is_prime(y):\n            prime_pairs.append([x, y])\n    return sorted(prime_pairs)\n\n# Function body without invoking the function:\ndef prime_number_pairs(n):\n    def is_prime(num):\n        if num <= 1:\n            return False\n        if num <= 3:\n            return True\n        if num % 2 == 0 or num % 3 == 0:\n            return False\n        i = 5\n        while i * i <= num:\n            if num % i == 0 or num % (i + 2) == 0:\n                return False\n            i += 6\n        return True\n    \n    prime_pairs = []\n    for x in range(2, n // 2 + 1):\n        y = n - x\n        if is_prime(x) and is_prime(y):\n            prime_pairs.append([x, y])\n    return sorted(prime_pairs)\n``` \n\nThe `prime_number_pairs` function includes a nested `is_prime` function to check if a number is prime. The main function then iterates through numbers from 2 to `n/2` to find pairs of prime numbers that sum up to `n`. It appends these pairs to the `prime_pairs` list and returns the sorted list without actually calling the function.",
