    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we can use dynamic programming. We will define a DP array `dp` where `dp[i]` represents the maximum amount of gold we can earn by selling houses from `0` to `i`. \n\nThe idea is to iterate over the offers and for each offer, calculate the maximum gold we can earn if we choose to sell the range of houses specified in the offer. We then update our `dp` array accordingly.\n\nHere is the Python function that would implement this logic:\n\n```python\ndef max_gold_earned(n, offers):\n    # Initialize a DP array with zeros, representing no gold earned up to that point\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n\n    # Sort offers by their starting point, and by their gold in descending order for the same start\n    offers.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], -x[2]))\n\n    # Process each offer\n    for start, end, gold in offers:\n        # Calculate the gold we earn if we take this offer\n        offer_earn = gold + dp[start]\n        # Update dp values in the range from end + 1 to n\n        for i in range(end + 1, n + 1):\n            dp[i] = max(dp[i], offer_earn)\n\n    # The maximum gold earned will be in dp[n]\n    return dp[n]\n\n# The function body is complete; it is not invoked here.\n```\n\nPlease note that this approach might not be the most efficient due to the nested loop for updating the `dp` array, and it could lead to a time complexity of O(n * m) where `m` is the number of offers. For very large inputs, a more efficient approach might be needed to handle the problem within a reasonable time frame.",
