      "generated_answer": "Here is the Python function body that would solve the given problem, without invoking the function:\n\n```python\ndef count_pairs_with_distance(coordinates, k):\n    # Initialize a dictionary to store the frequency of XOR results\n    xor_count = {}\n    # Initialize the count of pairs with the given distance\n    pair_count = 0\n    \n    # Iterate over each coordinate pair\n    for x, y in coordinates:\n        # Calculate the XOR of the current point with itself\n        xor_self = x ^ y\n        # Check if the XOR result minus k exists in the dictionary\n        if xor_self - k in xor_count:\n            # If it does, add the frequency to the pair_count\n            pair_count += xor_count[xor_self - k]\n        # Check if the XOR result plus k exists in the dictionary\n        if xor_self + k in xor_count:\n            # If it does, add the frequency to the pair_count\n            pair_count += xor_count[xor_self + k]\n        # Update the frequency of the current XOR result in the dictionary\n        xor_count[xor_self] = xor_count.get(xor_self, 0) + 1\n\n    # Return the total count of pairs with the given distance\n    return pair_count\n``` \n\nThis function uses a hash map to efficiently count the number of points whose XOR results would result in a pair with the given distance. It avoids the direct O(n^2) approach by counting the occurrences of XOR results that could form a pair with the current point under consideration.",
