      "generated_answer": "Here's the Python function that would solve the problem described:\n\n```python\ndef longest_semi_repetitive_substring(s):\n    n = len(s)\n    max_length = 1  # Minimum length of a semi-repetitive substring is 1\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + max_length, n + 1):  # Start checking from i + max_length to not repeat the same checks\n            substring = s[i:j]\n            if is_semi_repetitive(substring):\n                max_length = max(max_length, j - i)\n\n    return max_length\n\ndef is_semi_repetitive(substring):\n    # Check for at most one consecutive pair of the same digits\n    consecutive = False\n    for i in range(len(substring) - 1):\n        if substring[i] == substring[i + 1]:\n            if consecutive:  # If we already found a pair, it's not semi-repetitive\n                return False\n            consecutive = True\n    return True\n```\n\nThis function defines a helper function `is_semi_repetitive` that checks if a given substring has at most one consecutive pair of the same digit. The main function `longest_semi_repetitive_substring` iterates over all possible substrings of `s` and uses the helper function to determine if they are semi-repetitive, updating the maximum length found as needed. The approach uses a brute-force method to find the longest semi-repetitive substring, which is acceptable given the constraint (maximum length of `s` is 50).",
