      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nimport math\n\ndef find_farthest_points():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    N = int(data[0])\n    points = [(int(data[i*2+1]), int(data[i*2+2]), i+1) for i in range(N)]\n    \n    def euclidean_distance(p1, p2):\n        return math.sqrt((p1[0] - p2[0]) ** 2 + (p1[1] - p2[1]) ** 2)\n    \n    results = []\n    for i in range(N):\n        farthest_id = None\n        max_distance = -1\n        for j in range(N):\n            if i != j:\n                distance = euclidean_distance(points[i], points[j])\n                if distance > max_distance:\n                    max_distance = distance\n                    farthest_id = points[j][2]\n                elif distance == max_distance:\n                    if farthest_id is None or points[j][2] < farthest_id:\n                        farthest_id = points[j][2]\n        results.append(farthest_id)\n    \n    for result in results:\n        print(result)\n\n# The function find_farthest_points will be called when this script is executed with input redirection.\n```\n\nThis script defines a function `find_farthest_points` which reads from standard input, processes the data to find the farthest point for each point, and prints the results. The function reads all input at once, splits it into components, and processes each point to determine the farthest point using the Euclidean distance formula. It then prints the ID of the farthest point for each point in the input. This function is designed to be executed in an environment where input is provided via standard input redirection.",
