    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed array of positive integers nums.\nA subarray of nums is called incremovable if nums becomes strictly increasing on removing the subarray. For example, the subarray [3, 4] is an incremovable subarray of [5, 3, 4, 6, 7] because removing this subarray changes the array [5, 3, 4, 6, 7] to [5, 6, 7] which is strictly increasing.\nReturn the total number of incremovable subarrays of nums.\nNote that an empty array is considered strictly increasing.\nA subarray is a contiguous non-empty sequence of elements within an array.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,2,3,4]\nOutput: 10\nExplanation: The 10 incremovable subarrays are: [1], [2], [3], [4], [1,2], [2,3], [3,4], [1,2,3], [2,3,4], and [1,2,3,4], because on removing any one of these subarrays nums becomes strictly increasing. Note that you cannot select an empty subarray.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [6,5,7,8]\nOutput: 7\nExplanation: The 7 incremovable subarrays are: [5], [6], [5,7], [6,5], [5,7,8], [6,5,7] and [6,5,7,8].\nIt can be shown that there are only 7 incremovable subarrays in nums.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [8,7,6,6]\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: The 3 incremovable subarrays are: [8,7,6], [7,6,6], and [8,7,6,6]. Note that [8,7] is not an incremovable subarray because after removing [8,7] nums becomes [6,6], which is sorted in ascending order but not strictly increasing.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 50\n1 <= nums[i] <= 50",
    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we need to iterate over all possible subarrays of the given array and check if removing that subarray will result in a strictly increasing sequence in the remaining array. This requires a function that can:\n\n1. Iterate over all possible starting and ending indices of subarrays.\n2. Check if removing the subarray between these indices leaves the rest of the array strictly increasing.\n\nBelow is the Python function body that calculates the total number of incremovable subarrays:\n\n```python\ndef count_incremovable_subarrays(nums):\n    def is_strictly_increasing(arr):\n        for i in range(1, len(arr)):\n            if arr[i-1] >= arr[i]:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    def is_incremovable(subarray_start, subarray_end):\n        # Check if the array before the subarray is strictly increasing\n        if subarray_start > 0 and not is_strictly_increasing(nums[:subarray_start]):\n            return False\n        # Check if the array after the subarray is strictly increasing\n        if subarray_end < len(nums) - 1 and not is_strictly_increasing(nums[subarray_end+1:]):\n            return False\n        return True\n\n    count = 0\n    n = len(nums)\n    # Iterate over all possible subarray starts\n    for start in range(n):\n        # Iterate over all possible subarray ends\n        for end in range(start, n):\n            if is_incremovable(start, end):\n                count += 1\n    return count\n```\n\nThis function defines two inner helper functions, `is_strictly_increasing` to check if an array is strictly increasing, and `is_incremovable` to check if removing a subarray between two indices will leave the rest of the array strictly increasing. The outer function iterates over all possible subarrays, using these helper functions to count the number of incremovable subarrays.",
