    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_108",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed integer array nums of length n where n is the total number of students in the class. The class teacher tries to select a group of students so that all the students remain happy.\nThe i^th student will become happy if one of these two conditions is met:\n\nThe student is selected and the total number of selected students is strictly greater than nums[i].\nThe student is not selected and the total number of selected students is strictly less than nums[i].\n\nReturn the number of ways to select a group of students so that everyone remains happy.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,1]\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: \nThe two possible ways are:\nThe class teacher selects no student.\nThe class teacher selects both students to form the group. \nIf the class teacher selects just one student to form a group then the both students will not be happy. Therefore, there are only two possible ways.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [6,0,3,3,6,7,2,7]\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: \nThe three possible ways are:\nThe class teacher selects the student with index = 1 to form the group.\nThe class teacher selects the students with index = 1, 2, 3, 6 to form the group.\nThe class teacher selects all the students to form the group.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 10^5\n0 <= nums[i] < nums.length",
    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we can use a dynamic programming approach. We'll define a function `count_happy_groups` that takes an array `nums` as its argument. The function will iterate through possible group sizes and keep track of the number of ways to form groups such that all students are happy.\n\nHere is the function body without invoking it:\n\n```python\ndef count_happy_groups(nums):\n    # Maximum possible group size is the length of nums\n    max_group_size = len(nums)\n    \n    # Frequency array to count how many students want exactly k students in the group\n    frequency = [0] * (max_group_size + 1)\n    for num in nums:\n        frequency[num] += 1\n    \n    # Dynamic programming table to store the number of ways to form groups up to size i\n    dp = [0] * (max_group_size + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1  # Base case: There's one way to have a group of size 0 (select no students)\n\n    # Iterate over possible group sizes\n    for group_size in range(1, max_group_size + 1):\n        # Update dp table for the current group size\n        for student_size in range(group_size + 1):\n            # If student_size is in the frequency array, update the dp value\n            if student_size <= len(nums) and frequency[student_size] > 0:\n                dp[group_size] += dp[group_size - student_size] * frequency[student_size]\n    \n    # The result is the sum of all dp values where the group size is greater than the number of students who prefer that size\n    happy_groups_count = 0\n    for group_size in range(1, max_group_size + 1):\n        if group_size > len(nums) - frequency[group_size]:\n            happy_groups_count += dp[group_size]\n    \n    # Add the case where no students are selected\n    happy_groups_count += 1\n    \n    return happy_groups_count\n```\n\nThis function calculates the number of ways to select groups of students according to the given conditions. It first counts the frequency of each possible group size preference among the students, then uses dynamic programming to calculate the number of valid groups for each possible group size. Finally, it sums up the valid group counts and adds one for the case where no students are selected.",
