      "generated_answer": "To solve the problem of finding the maximum strength of a group of students, we can use a brute-force approach given the constraints (array length up to 13). We will generate all possible non-empty subsets of the array, calculate the product of the elements in each subset, and keep track of the maximum product found.\n\nHere is the Python function that implements this logic:\n\n```python\nfrom itertools import combinations\n\ndef max_strength_of_group(nums):\n    max_strength = float('-inf')  # Initialize to negative infinity to ensure any product is larger\n    \n    # Generate all possible non-empty subsets of nums\n    for r in range(1, len(nums) + 1):\n        for subset in combinations(nums, r):\n            # Calculate the product of the current subset\n            strength = 1\n            for num in subset:\n                strength *= num\n            \n            # Update max_strength if the current subset's strength is greater\n            if strength > max_strength:\n                max_strength = strength\n                \n    return max_strength\n```\n\nThis function uses the `combinations` function from Python's `itertools` module to generate all possible subsets of the input list `nums`. For each subset, it calculates the product of its elements and updates the `max_strength` if the current product is greater than the previously recorded maximum strength. The function finally returns the `max_strength`, which is the maximum strength of a group that can be formed.",
