orm two operations by choosing $i = 2$ and $i = 3$ in any order. After that, the array will be $a = [1, 6, 10]$. The product of the numbers in the array is $60$.",
    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\ninput = sys.stdin.read\n\ndef min_operations_to_make_product_divisible_by_k():\n    data = input().split()\n    index = 0\n    t = int(data[index])\n    index += 1\n    results = []\n    \n    for _ in range(t):\n        n, k = int(data[index]), int(data[index + 1])\n        index += 2\n        a = list(map(int, data[index:index + n]))\n        index += n\n        \n        # Count how many numbers are already divisible by k\n        count_divisible = sum(1 for x in a if x % k == 0)\n        \n        # If the count of divisible numbers is enough, no operations are needed\n        if count_divisible > 0:\n            results.append(0)\n            continue\n        \n        # Count the remainders when divided by k\n        remainder_count = [0] * k\n        for x in a:\n            remainder_count[x % k] += 1\n        \n        # Calculate the minimum operations needed\n        operations = 0\n        for i in range(1, k):\n            if remainder_count[i] > 0:\n                # If we need to make a[i] divisible by k, we need (k - i) operations\n                operations += (k - i) * remainder_count[i]\n        \n        results.append(operations)\n    \n    for result in results:\n        print(result)\n\n# Call the function to execute\nmin_operations_to_make_product_divisible_by_k()\n```",
