      "generated_answer": "To create a Python function that calculates the minimum number of swaps needed to make a permutation semi-ordered, we first need to identify the positions of the numbers 1 and n in the list. Then we can calculate the number of swaps needed to move 1 to the start of the list and n to the end of the list. Below is the body of the function that performs this operation:\n\n```python\ndef min_swaps_to_semi_ordered(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    # Initialize positions for 1 and n\n    pos_1 = pos_n = -1\n    \n    # Find positions of 1 and n\n    for i in range(n):\n        if nums[i] == 1:\n            pos_1 = i\n        elif nums[i] == n:\n            pos_n = i\n    \n    # If the permutation is already semi-ordered\n    if pos_1 == 0 and pos_n == n - 1:\n        return 0\n    \n    # Calculate swaps needed to make the permutation semi-ordered\n    swaps = 0\n    # Move 1 to the start (position 0)\n    swaps += pos_1\n    # Move n to the end (position n-1)\n    # We need to subtract 1 because we have already moved 1 to the start\n    swaps += (n - 1) - pos_n\n    \n    return swaps\n```\n\nThis function will correctly compute the minimum number of swaps required to make a given permutation semi-ordered, according to the constraints and examples provided.",
