      "generated_answer": "To solve the problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Parse the input to get the number of beans `N` and their deliciousness and color values.\n2. Group the beans by their color and determine the minimum deliciousness for each color.\n3. Find the maximum value among these minimum deliciousness values to determine the optimal choice.\n\nWe'll define a function called `max_min_deliciousness` which will encapsulate this logic. The function will read from standard input, process the data, and then print the result.\n\nHere is the Python function and its invocation:\n\n```python\ndef max_min_deliciousness():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    N = int(data[0])\n    beans = []\n    \n    index = 1\n    for _ in range(N):\n        A = int(data[index])\n        C = int(data[index + 1])\n        beans.append((A, C))\n        index += 2\n    \n    # Dictionary to hold the minimum deliciousness for each color\n    min_deliciousness_per_color = {}\n    \n    for deliciousness, color in beans:\n        if color not in min_deliciousness_per_color:\n            min_deliciousness_per_color[color] = deliciousness\n        else:\n            min_deliciousness_per_color[color] = min(min_deliciousness_per_color[color], deliciousness)\n    \n    # Find the maximum of the minimum deliciousness values\n    max_min = max(min_deliciousness_per_color.values())\n    \n    print(max_min)\n\n# Call the function\nmax_min_deliciousness()\n```\n\nThis function reads all input at once, which is suitable for competitive programming and scenarios where input size is large. It then processes the input to compute and print the desired output. The use of a dictionary to track the minimum deliciousness for each color allows us to efficiently group and find the necessary values.",
