      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we need to count the number of strings of length `n` that contain the substring \"leet\". Since the order of characters in \"leet\" can vary, we can treat this as a combinatorial problem. Specifically, we need to find the number of ways to arrange the characters in a string of length `n` that includes \"leet\" as a contiguous block, and then subtract this from the total number of strings of length `n` that only contain lowercase English letters.\n\nHowever, calculating this directly is complex due to the large constraints. Instead, we'll use dynamic programming to calculate the number of \"good\" strings.\n\nWe can define `dp[i][j]` as the number of good strings of length `i` where the last `j` characters are \"leet\". We will iterate over the possible lengths of the string, and for each length, we will calculate the number of good strings ending with \"leet\" by considering strings of smaller lengths.\n\nHere is the Python function that implements this idea:\n\n```python\ndef count_good_strings(n):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # Early return for small lengths where \"leet\" cannot be a substring\n    if n < 4:\n        return 0\n    \n    # The length of the substring \"leet\"\n    leet_length = 4\n    \n    # dp[i][j] will hold the number of good strings of length i where the last j characters are \"leet\"\n    dp = [[0] * (leet_length + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    \n    # Base case: 1 string of length 4 with the substring \"leet\" at the end\n    dp[4][4] = 1\n    \n    # Fill the dp table\n    for i in range(4, n + 1):\n        for j in range(1, leet_length + 1):\n            dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1]  # Extend strings of length i-1 with any character\n            \n            if j == leet_length:\n                dp[i][j] = (dp[i][j] + (26**(i - j) - sum(dp[i - j])) % MOD) % MOD\n    \n    # Sum up all good strings of length n\n    result = sum(dp[n]) % MOD\n    \n    return result\n```\n\nThis function calculates the number of good strings of length `n` by using dynamic programming. Note that the total number of strings of length `n` is `26^n`, and we need to subtract the number of strings that cannot be rearranged to contain \"leet\". We compute this subtraction in the inner loop where we update `dp[i][j]` by adding all strings of length `i - j` that are not good (`26**(i - j) - sum(dp[i - j])`) and then taking modulo `MOD` to ensure we do not exceed integer limits. The final result is the sum of all good strings of length `n`, also modulo `MOD`.",
