      "generated_answer": "Below is the Python function body that calculates the punishment number of a given positive integer `n` as per the definition provided:\n\n```python\ndef is_valid_partition(num_str, target):\n    # Helper function to check if the string representation of a number's square\n    # can be partitioned to sum up to the original number\n    for i in range(1, len(num_str)):\n        if sum(int(part) for part in num_str[:i]) == target:\n            return True\n    return sum(int(num_str)) == target\n\ndef punishment_number(n):\n    punishment_sum = 0\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        square = i * i\n        square_str = str(square)\n        if is_valid_partition(square_str, i):\n            punishment_sum += square\n    return punishment_sum\n```\n\nThis function defines a helper function `is_valid_partition` to check if the square of a number can be partitioned into parts that sum to the original number. The main function `punishment_number` iterates over all numbers from 1 to `n`, checks if the current number satisfies the punishment condition using the helper function, and if so, adds its square to the cumulative punishment sum, which is returned at the end.",
