    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given an integer array nums of length n, and a positive integer k.\nThe power of a subsequence is defined as the minimum absolute difference between any two elements in the subsequence.\nReturn the sum of powers of all subsequences of nums which have length equal to k.\nSince the answer may be large, return it modulo 10^9 + 7.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,2,3,4], k = 3\nOutput: 4\nExplanation:\nThere are 4 subsequences in nums which have length 3: [1,2,3], [1,3,4], [1,2,4], and [2,3,4]. The sum of powers is |2 - 3| + |3 - 4| + |2 - 1| + |3 - 4| = 4.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [2,2], k = 2\nOutput: 0\nExplanation:\nThe only subsequence in nums which has length 2 is [2,2]. The sum of powers is |2 - 2| = 0.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [4,3,-1], k = 2\nOutput: 10\nExplanation:\nThere are 3 subsequences in nums which have length 2: [4,3], [4,-1], and [3,-1]. The sum of powers is |4 - 3| + |4 - (-1)| + |3 - (-1)| = 10.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n2 <= n == nums.length <= 50\n-10^8 <= nums[i] <= 10^8 \n2 <= k <= n",
    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Here's the Python function body that calculates the sum of powers of all subsequences of a given length `k` from an array `nums`, and returns the result modulo \\(10^9 + 7\\):\n\n```python\ndef sum_of_powers(nums, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    n = len(nums)\n    nums.sort()\n    result = 0\n\n    # Helper function to calculate nCr (combinations of n items taken r at a time)\n    def nCr(n, r):\n        if r > n:\n            return 0\n        f = 1\n        for i in range(1, r + 1):\n            f = f * (n - i + 1) // i\n        return f\n\n    # Iterate over all possible starting points for subsequences of length k\n    for i in range(n - k + 1):\n        # Iterate over all possible ending points for subsequences of length k\n        for j in range(i + k - 1, n):\n            # Calculate the power of the current subsequence\n            power = nums[j] - nums[i]\n            # Calculate the number of such subsequences\n            count = nCr(j - i, k - 1)\n            # Add to the result the product of the power and the number of such subsequences\n            result = (result + power * count) % MOD\n\n    return result\n```\n\nThis function first sorts the array to ensure that subsequences are considered in non-decreasing order, which is important because the minimum absolute difference between any two elements in a sorted subsequence is simply the difference between consecutive elements. It then iterates over all possible starting and ending points for subsequences of length `k`, calculates the power of the subsequence, and adds it to the result after considering the number of such subsequences using a combination calculation. The result is returned modulo \\(10^9 + 7\\) as required.",
