      "generated_answer": "Here's the Python function body that solves the given problem:\n\n```python\ndef shortest_subarray_with_sum(nums, target):\n    # Initialize the prefix sum array and the minimum length to infinity\n    prefix_sum = [0]\n    for num in nums:\n        prefix_sum.append(prefix_sum[-1] + num)\n\n    # Initialize the hashmap to store the prefix sum indices\n    prefix_sum_indices = {0: -1}\n    min_length = float('inf')\n\n    # Iterate through the prefix sums to find the shortest subarray\n    for i, s in enumerate(prefix_sum):\n        if s - target in prefix_sum_indices:\n            min_length = min(min_length, i - prefix_sum_indices[s - target])\n        prefix_sum_indices[s] = i\n\n    # If min_length was updated, return it; otherwise, return -1\n    return min_length if min_length != float('inf') else -1\n```\n\nThis function uses a sliding window approach with the help of a hashmap to track the prefix sums. It calculates the prefix sums of the given `nums` array and uses these sums to determine the shortest subarray that sums to `target`. If such a subarray exists, it returns the length; otherwise, it returns -1. Note that the actual infinite nature of the `infinite_nums` is simulated by the fact that we are looking for a subarray within the bounds of the given `nums array, because any subarray in `infinite_nums` is also a subarray in repeated `nums`.",
