      "generated_answer": "Here is the Python function body that calculates the minimum possible sum of the cost of dividing an array into 3 disjoint contiguous subarrays:\n\n```python\ndef min_cost_of_dividing(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    prefix_sum = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(n):\n        prefix_sum[i + 1] = prefix_sum[i] + nums[i]\n\n    min_cost = float('inf')\n\n    # Iterate through possible first and second cut positions\n    for i in range(1, n - 1):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            # Calculate cost of the 3 subarrays\n            cost = nums[0] + nums[i] + nums[j]\n            min_cost = min(min_cost, cost)\n\n    return min_cost\n```\n\nThis function computes the minimum cost by iterating through all possible positions for the first and second cuts to create three contiguous subarrays. It uses a prefix sum array to quickly calculate the sum of any subarray. The outer two loops try every possible combination of first and second cut positions (`i` and `j`), and for each combination, it calculates the cost of the resulting three subarrays, updating the minimum cost found so far. Note that the cost of each subarray is simply the value of its first element, as per the problem's definition.",
