    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed integer array nums, and you are allowed to traverse between its indices. You can traverse between index i and index j, i != j, if and only if gcd(nums[i], nums[j]) > 1, where gcd is the greatest common divisor.\nYour task is to determine if for every pair of indices i and j in nums, where i < j, there exists a sequence of traversals that can take us from i to j.\nReturn true if it is possible to traverse between all such pairs of indices, or false otherwise.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [2,3,6]\nOutput: true\nExplanation: In this example, there are 3 possible pairs of indices: (0, 1), (0, 2), and (1, 2).\nTo go from index 0 to index 1, we can use the sequence of traversals 0 -> 2 -> 1, where we move from index 0 to index 2 because gcd(nums[0], nums[2]) = gcd(2, 6) = 2 > 1, and then move from index 2 to index 1 because gcd(nums[2], nums[1]) = gcd(6, 3) = 3 > 1.\nTo go from index 0 to index 2, we can just go directly because gcd(nums[0], nums[2]) = gcd(2, 6) = 2 > 1. Likewise, to go from index 1 to index 2, we can just go directly because gcd(nums[1], nums[2]) = gcd(3, 6) = 3 > 1.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [3,9,5]\nOutput: false\nExplanation: No sequence of traversals can take us from index 0 to index 2 in this example. So, we return false.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [4,3,12,8]\nOutput: true\nExplanation: There are 6 possible pairs of indices to traverse between: (0, 1), (0, 2), (0, 3), (1, 2), (1, 3), and (2, 3). A valid sequence of traversals exists for each pair, so we return true.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 10^5\n1 <= nums[i] <= 10^5",
    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we can model the relationship between indices as a graph where each index is a node and an edge exists between nodes `i` and `j` if `gcd(nums[i], nums[j]) > 1`. The task is then to check if this graph is connected, meaning that there is a path between any two nodes.\n\nThe function to check connectivity can be implemented using a union-find data structure (also known as disjoint-set) or by performing a depth-first search (DFS) or breadth-first search (BFS) from any node and ensuring all nodes are visited.\n\nBelow is the Python function body that checks if the graph is connected using DFS:\n\n```python\nfrom math import gcd\n\ndef is_connected(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    if n <= 1:\n        return True\n\n    # Helper function to perform DFS\n    def dfs(node, visited):\n        stack = [node]\n        while stack:\n            current = stack.pop()\n            for neighbor in range(n):\n                if not visited[neighbor] and gcd(nums[current], nums[neighbor]) > 1:\n                    visited[neighbor] = True\n                    stack.append(neighbor)\n\n    # Initialize visited array\n    visited = [False] * n\n\n    # Start DFS from the first node\n    visited[0] = True\n    dfs(0, visited)\n\n    # Check if all nodes are visited\n    return all(visited)\n\n# Function to determine if traversal is possible between all pairs of indices\ndef can_traverse_all_pairs(nums):\n    # Call the is_connected function and return the result\n    return is_connected(nums)\n```\n\nThis function `can_traverse_all_pairs` takes an array of integers `nums` and returns a boolean indicating whether it is possible to traverse between all pairs of indices as per the given conditions. The `is_connected` function is responsible for checking the connectivity of the graph using a DFS approach. It assumes that all indices can be reached from the starting index (0 in this case), and if any index is not reachable, the function would return `False`, indicating that not all pairs can be traversed. If all indices are reachable from the starting index, it returns `True`.",
