    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we can use dynamic programming with bitmasking. Here is the Python function body that calculates the number of special permutations according to the given prompt:\n\n```python\ndef countSpecialPermutations(nums):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    n = len(nums)\n    dp = [{} for _ in range(1 << n)]  # dp[mask] will store the count of special permutations ending at each number\n\n    # Initialize dp for the base case where only one number is included in the permutation\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[1 << i][nums[i]] = 1\n\n    # Iterate over all possible masks (permutations)\n    for mask in range(1, 1 << n):\n        for i in range(n):\n            if mask & (1 << i):  # if the i-th number is included in the permutation\n                # Find the previous mask by removing the i-th bit\n                prev_mask = mask & ~(1 << i)\n                if prev_mask == 0:\n                    continue\n\n                # Iterate over all numbers in the previous mask\n                for num in dp[prev_mask]:\n                    # Check if the current number and the number from the previous mask\n                    # are in a special relationship\n                    if num % nums[i] == 0 or nums[i] % num == 0:\n                        # Add the count from the previous mask to the current mask\n                        dp[mask][nums[i]] = (dp[mask].get(nums[i], 0) + dp[prev_mask][num]) % MOD\n\n    # Sum up all the counts in the dp array for the full mask (all numbers included)\n    return sum(dp[(1 << n) - 1].values()) % MOD\n```\n\nThis function uses a bitmask to represent subsets of the array and a dictionary to store counts of special permutations ending with a specific number for each subset. The loop iterates over all possible subsets (masks) and for each subset, it considers each number in the subset as the last element in the permutation. Then, it checks all the previous permutations (represented by the previous mask) to see if they can form a special relationship with the current number. If they can, it updates the count for the current permutation. At the end, it sums up the counts for the permutations that include all the numbers and returns the result modulo \\(10^9 + 7\\).",
