      "generated_answer": "To solve the problem, we need to understand the operations and the pattern of costs incurred by Takahashi. Each integer x on the blackboard contributes to the total cost by x yen when it is chosen for the operation. The operation then generates two new integers from x, which are \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{2} \\right\\rfloor\\) and \\(\\left\\lceil \\frac{x}{2} \\right\\rceil\\). The process continues until all integers on the blackboard are less than 2.\n\nThe key observation is that each operation effectively doubles the number of integers (or more specifically, the cost contribution) at a reduced rate, and the total cost is accumulated based on the initial value of x and its contributions through the operations.\n\nThe solution can be approached by understanding the binary representation of the number N. Each bit in the binary representation of N contributes to the total cost in a way that can be calculated using powers of 2. The cost contributed by each bit can be determined by its position in the binary representation.\n\nHere's the Python function to compute the total cost:\n\n```python\ndef calculate_total_cost():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    n = int(input().strip())\n    \n    # Initialize the total cost\n    total_cost = 0\n    \n    # We will calculate the cost based on the power of 2 contributions\n    power_of_two = 1\n    while n > 0:\n        # If the current bit is set (i.e., n is odd), add the current power of two to the total cost\n        if n % 2 == 1:\n            total_cost += power_of_two\n        \n        # Move to the next bit\n        n //= 2\n        power_of_two *= 2\n    \n    # Output the total cost\n    print(total_cost)\n\n# Call the function\ncalculate_total_cost()\n```\n\nThis function reads an integer from standard input, calculates the total cost based on the binary representation of the number, and prints the result. The loop iterates over each bit of the number, checking if it's set (i.e., contributes to the cost) and then updates the total cost accordingly. The power of two is updated in each iteration to reflect the position of the bit in the binary representation.",
