    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve the problem of partitioning an array into two non-empty subarrays such that the absolute difference between the maximum of the first subarray and the minimum of the second subarray is minimized, we can use a greedy approach. The key insight is that sorting the array allows us to efficiently find the optimal partition point.\n\nHere's the Python function that accomplishes this:\n\n```python\ndef min_partition_value(nums):\n    # Sort the array to facilitate the partitioning\n    nums.sort()\n    \n    # Initialize variables to store the minimum value of the second subarray\n    # and the maximum value of the first subarray as we iterate through the sorted array\n    min_second_subarray = float('inf')\n    max_first_subarray = float('-inf')\n    \n    # Iterate through the array to find the optimal partition point\n    for i in range(len(nums) - 1):\n        max_first_subarray = max(max_first_subarray, nums[i])\n        min_second_subarray = min(min_second_subarray, nums[i + 1])\n        \n        # Calculate the current partition value\n        current_partition_value = abs(max_first_subarray - min_second_subarray)\n    \n    # The minimum partition value found during the iteration\n    return current_partition_value\n```\n\nThis function first sorts the input array `nums`. It then iterates through the sorted array, maintaining the maximum value found so far for the first subarray (`max_first_subarray`) and the minimum value found so far for the second subarray (`min_second_subarray`). At each step, it calculates the partition value and keeps track of the minimum partition value encountered. Finally, it returns the minimum partition value. This approach ensures that we find the optimal partitioning efficiently.",
