    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9136",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Say the incidence of a disease D is about 5 cases per 100 people (i.e., P(D) = 0.05). Let Boolean random variable D mean a patient “has disease D” and let Boolean random variable TP stand for \"tests positive.\" Tests for disease D are known to be very accurate in the sense that the probability of testing positive when you have the disease is 0.99, and the probability of testing negative when you do not have the disease is 0.97. What is P(D | TP), the posterior probability that you have disease D when the test is positive?\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.0495\nB. 0.02\nC. 0.05\nD. 0.1\nE. 0.99\nF. 0.635\nG. 0.078\nH. 0.25\nI. 0.97\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is \\boxed{F}. To find \\( P(D | TP) \\), we use Bayes' theorem, which states that \\( P(D | TP) = \\frac{P(TP | D) \\cdot P(D)}{P(TP)} \\). Given that \\( P(TP | D) = 0.99 \\) and \\( P(D) = 0.05 \\), we also need \\( P(TP) \\), the total probability of testing positive, which is \\( P(TP) = P(TP | D) \\cdot P(D) + P(TP | \\neg D) \\cdot P(\\neg D) \\), where \\( P(TP | \\neg D) = 1 - P(\\neg TP | \\neg D) = 1 - 0.97 = 0.03 \\) and \\( P(\\neg D) = 1 - P(D) = 0.95 \\). Plugging these values into the equation gives us \\( P(D | TP) = \\frac{0.99 \\cdot 0.05}{0.99 \\cdot 0.05 + 0.03 \\cdot 0.95} \\approx 0.635 \\).",
