    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{D}\n\nThe normalized throughput \\( S \\) in a slotted Aloha network can be calculated using the formula \\( S = G \\cdot p \\cdot (1 - p) \\), where \\( G \\) is the offered load and \\( p \\) is the probability that a station transmits in a given slot. For three stations, \\( G = p_A + p_B + p_C = 0.2 + 0.3 + 0.4 = 0.9 \\). The probability \\( p \\) of any station transmitting is the sum of their individual probabilities, so \\( p = 0.9 \\). The normalized throughput is thus \\( S = 0.9 \\cdot 0.9 \\cdot (1 - 0.9) = 0.27 \\cdot 0.1 = 0.027 \\), but the question seems to imply we are looking for the sum of the probabilities since throughput in a slotted Aloha is often expressed as a ratio of successful transmissions to the total slots (not normalized by the slot time). Therefore, the sum of the probabilities \\( p_A + p_B + p_C = 0.9 \\) corresponds to choice D, which is the closest to the sum of the individual probabilities.",
