      "generated_answer": "The Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) is the ratio of the peak voltage of the signal to the peak voltage of the noise. If the peak voltage of the signal is 20 times the peak voltage of the noise, the SNR is 20. To convert the SNR to decibels (SNR$_{\\text{dB}}$), we use the formula SNR$_{\\text{dB}}$ = 20 * log$_{10}$(SNR). Plugging in 20 for SNR gives us SNR$_{\\text{dB}}$ = 20 * log$_{10}$(20), which is approximately 26.0 dB. \n\n\\boxed{E}",
