    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9450",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a CSMA/CD network with a data rate of 10 Mbps, the minimum frame size is found to be 512 bits for the correct operation of the collision detection process. What should be the minimum frame size (in bits) if we increase the data rate to 1 Gbps?\n\nOptions: \nA. 25600\nB. 10000\nC. 102400\nD. 32000\nE. 51200\nF. 6400\nG. 204800\nH. 256000\nI. 409600\nJ. 12800\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}\n\nThe minimum frame size in a CSMA/CD network is determined by the time it takes for a frame to reach the farthest station on the network, which must be enough time for a collision to be detected before the entire frame is transmitted. This is often referred to as the \"slot time\". When the data rate increases, the slot time decreases because the same amount of data can be transmitted in less time. To maintain the same slot time, the frame size must increase proportionally to the increase in data rate. Since the data rate increases from 10 Mbps to 1 Gbps (100 times increase), the minimum frame size must also increase by 100 times. Therefore, the minimum frame size should be \\( 512 \\text{ bits} \\times 100 = 51200 \\text{ bits} \\). The correct choice is C.",
      "success": true,
