    "global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_1892",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Have studies on learning supported the Incremental or the One-trial Theory of learning?\n\nOptions: \nA. Incremental Theory is supported in cognitive tasks, while One-trial Theory is supported in motor tasks.\nB. The Incremental Theory is universally supported, while the One-trial Theory has been completely discredited.\nC. Studies support the One-trial Theory in the short term, but the Incremental Theory in the long term.\nD. Studies support a hybrid model that combines elements of both theories.\nE. One-trial Theory is supported for simple tasks, while Incremental Theory is for complex tasks.\nF. Neither theories of learning are correct.\nG. Both theories of learning are correct, depending on the context.\nH. The Incremental Theory is only supported in animal studies, while the One-trial Theory is supported in human studies.\nI. Only One-trial Theory of learning is supported.\nJ. Only Incremental Theory of learning is supported.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{G}\n\nBoth the Incremental Theory and One-trial Theory of learning have been supported by studies in different contexts. The Incremental Theory suggests that learning is a gradual process, which is supported by research in various cognitive and motor tasks that show improvement over time. The One-trial Theory posits that learning can occur in a single instance, which is supported in situations where rapid learning is observed, such as in classical conditioning or certain types of pavlovian learning. Therefore, the context-dependent nature of learning supports both theories.",
