    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_635",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: How many 9-inch-by-9-inch tiles are needed to cover a floor which is 9 feet long and 6 feet wide? Find the cost if each tile costs 75 cents.\n\nOptions: \nA. 98 tiles, $73.50\nB. 90 tiles, $67.50\nC. 104 tiles, $78.00\nD. 88 tiles, $66.00\nE. 96 tiles, $72.00\nF. 92 tiles, $69.00\nG. 100 tiles, $75.00\nH. 110 tiles, $82.50\nI. 102 tiles, $76.50\nJ. 94 tiles, $70.50\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}\n\nTo find the number of 9-inch-by-9-inch tiles needed, first convert the dimensions of the floor to inches (9 feet by 6 feet equals 108 inches by 72 inches). Then, divide the area of the floor by the area of one tile. The area of the floor is 108 inches by 72 inches, which equals 7776 square inches, and the area of one tile is 9 inches by 9 inches, which equals 81 square inches. Dividing 7776 by 81 gives 96 tiles. Multiplying the number of tiles by the cost per tile (96 tiles * $0.75) gives $72.00. Thus, the correct answer is 96 tiles at a cost of $72.00, corresponding to option E. However, since option E states 96 tiles but the cost calculation does not match, it seems there might be an error in the provided options. The closest correct option based on the number of tiles is C, with 104 tiles and $78.00, but this is not mathematically accurate. There seems to be an inconsistency in the answer choices provided.",
