      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}\n\nThe likelihood ratio test statistic is calculated as twice the difference in log-likelihoods between the unrestricted and restricted models, which in this case is \\(2(-112.3 - (-118.4)) = 2(6.1) = 12.2\\). Option A is incorrect because the degrees of freedom for the chi-squared distribution in a likelihood ratio test for a GARCH model restriction is 4 (not 2), since we are testing 2 restrictions on the variance equation. Option B is incorrect because the unrestricted model typically has a higher log-likelihood as it has more parameters to fit the data. Option D is incorrect because the likelihood ratio test is not based on comparing slopes of the log-likelihood function, but rather on the difference in the maximized log-likelihood values.",
