by a deed that contained no mention of the driveway located on lot 1. The neighbor and the professor assumed that the latter had the right to use the driveway, so they didn't insert any recitations in their deed instrument regarding the driveway. Immediately upon her taking possession of the premises, the daughter began to use the driveway on lot 1. Consequently, she objected to the professor's use of the driveway. After the daughter refused to permit the professor to use the driveway, he brought suit to determine his right to continue use of the driveway. The professor should\n\nOptions: \nA. win, because the neighbor's easement to use the driveway was conveyed to the professor.\nB. lose, because the original deed granting use of the driveway specified it could only be used by the neighbor, not his successors or assigns.\nC. lose, because the deed instrument between the co-worker, boss, and the neighbor was never recorded.\nD. win, because the professor was under the assumption that he had rights to use the driveway.\nE. win, because the deed to lot 2 implicitly included the use of the driveway on lot 1.\nF. lose, because the neighbor's non-use of the driveway effectuated an abandonment of the easement.\nG. win, because he acquired an implied easement to use the driveway as owner of the dominant tenement.\nH. lose, because the Statute of Frauds was not satisfied.\nI. win, because the driveway was considered a shared asset among the adjacent lots.\nJ. lose, because the daughter of the boss has exclusive rights to the property, including the driveway, upon her mother's death.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
