      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{F}\n\nThe strongest constitutional grounds against the authority of the statute would be based on the principle of federalism, as seen in option F. The U.S. Constitution's Tenth Amendment reserves powers not delegated to the federal government to the states, and requiring states to enforce federal regulations could be seen as a violation of states' rights. This argument is stronger than the others because it directly challenges the constitutional balance of power between federal and state governments.",
