      "generated_answer": "To find the values of $x$ where $f(x) = \\sin(7\\pi \\cdot \\sin(5x)) = 0$, we need $\\sin(7\\pi \\cdot \\sin(5x)) = 0$. This occurs when $7\\pi \\cdot \\sin(5x)$ is an integer multiple of $\\pi$, i.e., $7\\pi \\cdot \\sin(5x) = k\\pi$ for some integer $k$. Simplifying gives $\\sin(5x) = \\frac{k}{7}$.\n\nSince $0 < x < 2\\pi$, the function $\\sin(5x)$ will complete 5 full cycles within this interval. For each cycle, there are 14 points where $\\sin(5x)$ crosses the x-axis (0, ±1/7, ±2/7, ..., ±1), but only 4 points per cycle where $\\sin(5x)$ is an integer multiple of $\\pi/7$ (since $k$ must be an integer and there are 7 possible values for $k$). Thus, there are $4 \\times 5 = 20$ values of $x$ where $f(x) = 0$.\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is tangent to the $x$-axis where $f(x) = 0$ and $f'(x) = 0$. However, since $f'(x)$ involves a composition of trigonometric functions, there are no additional points where the derivative is zero within the given conditions. Therefore, $t = 20$.\n\nThe sum $n + t$ is then $20 + 20 = 40$.\n\n\\boxed{40}",
