    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the probability that the reciprocal of a randomly selected positive odd integer less than 2010 gives a terminating decimal, we need to find how many of these integers have a denominator that only consists of the prime factors 2 and 5, since only fractions with these factors in the denominator terminate. Among the positive odd integers less than 2010, only those that are multiples of 5 (and not multiples of 10, which are even) will have terminating decimals when their reciprocals are taken. There are 2010/5 = 402 multiples of 5 less than 2010, but we must subtract the even multiples of 5 (multiples of 10), which are half of these, or 2010/10 = 201. So, there are 402 - 201 = 201 odd multiples of 5. The total number of positive odd integers less than 2010 is (2010-1)/2 = 1004. Thus, the probability is 201/1004, which simplifies to 1/5 because both the numerator and the denominator are divisible by 201. The relatively prime positive integers a and b are 1 and 5, respectively, so a+b = 1+5 = 6.\n\n\\boxed{6}",
