      "generated_answer": "To determine the domain of the function $f(x) = \\frac{{2-x}}{{\\log(2-\\log(x-2))}}$, we need to consider where the logarithm function is defined and where the denominator is not zero. The argument of the logarithm, $2 - \\log(x-2)$, must be greater than zero, and the base $10$ logarithm is defined only for positive arguments. This gives us two conditions:\n\n1. $2 - \\log(x-2) > 0$ which implies $\\log(x-2) < 2$.\n2. The denominator $\\log(2-\\log(x-2))$ cannot be zero.\n\nFrom the first condition, we have $x-2 < 10^2$, which simplifies to $x < 102$. Also, since $x-2$ must be positive for the logarithm to be defined, we have $x > 2$. From the second condition, $\\log(2-\\log(x-2)) \\neq 0$ implies $2-\\log(x-2) \\neq 10^0$, which simplifies to $2-\\log(x-2) \\neq 1$. This further implies $\\log(x-2) \\neq 1$, which simplifies to $x-2 \\neq 10^1$, or $x \\neq 12$.\n\nCombining these, the domain of $f(x)$ is $2 < x < 12$ or $12 < x < 102$, which in interval notation is $\\boxed{(2, 12) \\cup (12, 102)}$.",
