    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Nerve outgrowth from a developing neuron begins at the growth cone, located at the tip of the axon. Microspikes of the growth cone extend and retract in order to move the growth cone forward. Exposure of the neuron to cytochasalin B at this stage of development causes\n\nOptions: \nA. microtubules in the growth cone to undergo reversible dissociation\nB. microtubules in the axon to undergo irreversible dissociation\nC. microfilaments in the growth cone to undergo irreversible depolymerization\nD. microfilaments in the axon to undergo reversible depolymerization\nE. microfilaments in the microspike to undergo reversible depolymerization\nF. microtubules in the microspike to undergo irreversible depolymerization\nG. microfilaments in the axon to undergo irreversible depolymerization\nH. microtubules in the microspike to undergo reversible depolymerization\nI. microfilaments in the microspike to undergo irreversible depolymerization\nJ. microtubules in the axon to undergo reversible dissociation\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}\n\nCytochalasin B is known to disrupt the polymerization of actin filaments (microfilaments) by binding to the growing end of the filament, leading to depolymerization. Since the growth cone of a neuron is rich in microfilaments that are crucial for its movement and cytochalasin B affects microfilaments, the most likely correct answer is that microfilaments in the growth cone would undergo irreversible depolymerization. This matches option C.",
