      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}\n\nThe placenta secretes a hormone called human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) which takes the place of LH in preserving the corpus luteum. This allows the corpus luteum to continue secreting high levels of progesterone necessary for maintaining the uterine lining during the early stages of pregnancy until the placenta itself takes over progesterone production. This mechanism ensures that the corpus luteum is not prematurely atrophied, allowing the pregnancy to continue.",
