    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The uncertainty of the momentum can be calculated using the Heisenberg uncertainty principle, which states that the product of the uncertainties in position and momentum is at least equal to Planck's constant divided by 2. Given that the uncertainty in position (\\(\\Delta x\\)) is \\(10 \\mathrm{pm} = 10 \\times 10^{-12} \\mathrm{m}\\), and Planck's constant (\\(h\\)) is approximately \\(6.626 \\times 10^{-34} \\mathrm{Js}\\), we can find the uncertainty in momentum (\\(\\Delta p\\)) as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta p \\geq \\frac{h}{2\\Delta x}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Delta p \\geq \\frac{6.626 \\times 10^{-34}}{2 \\times 10 \\times 10^{-12}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Delta p \\geq 3.313 \\times 10^{-23} \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot \\mathrm{m} \\cdot \\mathrm{s}^{-1}\n\\]\n\nThus, the uncertainty in the momentum of the electron is at least \\(3.313 \\times 10^{-23} \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot \\mathrm{m} \\cdot \\mathrm{s}^{-1}\\). The closest value to this from the options given is option D: 3.3 \\(10^{-23} \\mathrm{~kg} \\cdot \\mathrm{m} \\cdot \\mathrm{s}^{-1}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{D}\n\\]",
