    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_3983",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The duration of a $90^{{\\circ}}$ or $180^{{\\circ}}$ pulse depends on the strength of the $\\mathscr{{B}}_1$ field. If a $180^{{\\circ}}$ pulse requires $12.5 \\mu \\mathrm{{s}}$, what is the strength of the $\\mathscr{{B}}_1$ field? \n\nOptions: \nA. 3.8 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nB. 4.2 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nC. 4.7 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nD. 6.2 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nE. 7.1 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nF. 7.8 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nG. 9.3 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nH.  5.9 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nI. 5.0 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nJ. 8.4 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the strength of the $\\mathscr{B}_1$ field, we use the relationship between the pulse duration, the flip angle, and the $\\mathscr{B}_1$ field strength. The formula to calculate the strength of the $\\mathscr{B}_1$ field is given by:\n\n\\[ \\mathscr{B}_1 = \\frac{\\gamma \\Delta \\omega}{2\\pi} \\]\n\nwhere $\\gamma$ is the gyromagnetic ratio of the nucleus being excited, $\\Delta \\omega$ is the angular frequency difference between the equilibrium and the excited state, which is related to the pulse duration $T$ by $\\Delta \\omega = \\frac{2\\pi}{T}$. For a $180^{\\circ}$ pulse, the angular frequency difference is twice that of a $90^{\\circ}$ pulse.\n\nHowever, the provided options do not include the necessary information about $\\gamma$ to calculate $\\mathscr{B}_1$ directly. Typically, the gyromagnetic ratio for protons (which is often used in NMR and MRI contexts) is approximately $2.675 \\times 10^8 \\, \\text{rad} \\cdot \\text{s}^{-1} \\cdot \\text{T}^{-1}$. Using this value and rearranging the formula to solve for $\\mathscr{B}_1$:\n\n\\[ \\mathscr{B}_1 = \\frac{2 \\times 2.675 \\times 10^8 \\, \\text{rad} \\cdot \\text{s}^{-1} \\cdot \\text{T}^{-1} \\times \\frac{2\\pi}{12.5 \\times 10^{-6} \\, \\text{s}}}{2\\pi} \\]\n\n\\[ \\mathscr{B}_1 = \\frac{2 \\times 2.675 \\times 10^8 \\times 2\\pi}{12.5 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 2\\pi} \\]\n\n\\[ \\mathscr{B}_1 = \\frac{2 \\times 2.675 \\times 10^8}{12.5 \\times 10^{-6}} \\]\n\n\\[ \\mathscr{B}_1 = 4.34 \\times 10^{-4} \\, \\text{T} \\]\n\nThe closest answer to $4.34 \\times 10^{-4} \\, \\text{T}$ from the provided options is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{C} \\] (4.7 $10^{-4}$ $\\mathrm{T}$)",
