    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{D}\n\nThe degree of $f(g(x))$ is the degree of $f$ composed with $g$, which means multiplying the degrees of $f$ and $g$. So, $f(g(x))$ has degree $6 \\cdot 3 = 18$. Similarly, the degree of $h(f(x))$ would be the degree of $h$ composed with $f$. Since the highest degree polynomial resulting from $f(g(x)) + g(h(x)) + h(f(x))$ is $36$, and the degrees of $f(g(x))$ and $h(f(x))$ are both multiples of 6 (since they are the product of a degree-6 polynomial with another polynomial), the degree of $h$ must be a multiple of 6 that, when multiplied by 6 (the degree of $f$), gives us 36. The only such multiple of 6 that is less than or equal to 36 is 6 itself, which would make $h(f(x))$ have a degree of $6 \\cdot 6 = 36$. However, this would leave no room for the degree of $g(h(x))$, which would also need to be a multiple of 6. The only way to satisfy the condition that the sum of the degrees is 36, while each term is a multiple of 6, is if $h$ has a degree of 9, which would make $h(f(x))$ have a degree of $9 \\cdot 6 = 54$, but since $g(h(x))$ has to have a degree that is a multiple of 3, the only possible degree for $h$ that could work in this context is 9. Therefore, the degree of the polynomial $h$ is 9.",
