      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{I}\n\nThe speed of longitudinal (sound) waves in a wire is determined by the equation \\( v_l = \\sqrt{\\frac{T}{\\mu}} \\), where \\( T \\) is the tension and \\( \\mu \\) is the linear mass density of the wire. The speed of transverse waves in a wire is given by \\( v_t = \\sqrt{\\frac{T}{\\mu}} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{\\rho}} \\), where \\( \\rho \\) is the density of the wire. For the speeds to be the same, the Young's modulus \\( E \\) and the shear modulus \\( G \\) must be related by \\( G = \\frac{E}{2(1 + \\nu)} \\), where \\( \\nu \\) is the Poisson's ratio. Since the Poisson's ratio for materials is always less than 1, the speeds of the waves cannot be made equal by tension alone. Therefore, this situation is physically unrealizable.",
